E=[
('01-00','Investment and aid','Foreign investment places resources in an economy from abroad with an investment interest or return; aid provides assistance for development or humanitarian needs.','Aid can include grants, expertise, goods or concessional finance. Purpose and terms differ; foreign money is not automatically a gift.'),
('01-01','Foreign investment','Foreign investment is investment by an investor based outside the recipient economy.','Direct investment involves lasting interest and influence in an enterprise; other holdings may be financial investments without the same productive control.'),
('01-02','Aid','Aid is assistance intended to support development or humanitarian needs, through resources such as grants, expertise, goods or suitable finance.','A grant need not be repaid, while a loan carries repayment terms. Assess delivery, purpose and conditions separately.'),
('02-00','Forms and conditions','Investment, grants, technical assistance, loans and credits have different ownership, repayment and delivery arrangements.','A loan is a borrowing arrangement; debt is the amount outstanding. Emergency or development purpose differs from bilateral delivery between countries or multilateral delivery through institutions.'),
('02-01','Direct investment and companies','Foreign direct investment can establish productive facilities and connect technology or markets through firms, including multinationals.','Local skills, suppliers and retained benefits matter; finance and jobs can coexist with profits leaving or environmental costs.'),
('02-02','Loans and credits','Foreign loans provide resources with repayment obligations, often with interest and agreed timing.','They may finance useful capacity but create debt-service needs. Borrowing for an unusable asset adds obligations without the expected earning or service benefit.'),
('03-00','Institutions have distinct roles','The syllabus groups several international development bodies, but their mandates and institutional forms differ.','The World Bank and IMF are international financial institutions, UNDP a development programme and UNICEF a children’s fund; FAO and UNESCO are specialised agencies. Match the actual need and role rather than calling them interchangeable donors.'),
('03-01','World Bank','The World Bank supports development through finance, knowledge and technical assistance, including long-term projects and institutional capacity.','A proposed infrastructure or service project needs feasible design and implementation; institutional support does not prove the project’s outcomes.'),
('03-02','IMF','The IMF supports monetary cooperation and economic stability through monitoring, advice, capacity development and lending for balance-of-payments difficulties.','Its stabilisation role differs from simply building schools or giving unconditional grants; lending conditions and effects require case evidence.'),
('03-03','UNDP','UNDP works on development including poverty reduction, governance, resilience and environmental sustainability through partnerships and technical support.','It can help strengthen capabilities and coordination; a broad mandate does not mean it performs every service itself.'),
('03-04','UNICEF','UNICEF focuses on children’s rights and well-being, including health, nutrition, education, water and protection.','Child-focused assistance can link several needs, while implementation and reach still require evidence.'),
('03-05','FAO','FAO works on food security, agriculture and related knowledge and cooperation to address hunger.','Agricultural advice and evidence can support suitable production, but need local access, capabilities and conditions to become useful.'),
('03-06','UNESCO','UNESCO promotes international cooperation in education, science, culture and information.','Its education and heritage work differs from general lending or food-distribution functions; select the role relevant to the development question.')]
