P={'P01':(['01-00','02-00'],'State (a) the difference between grant and loan repayment terms [2]. Explain (b) why a fictional large loan for an unused machine with repayments due can be weaker than smaller assistance addressing a working producer’s missing input [4].','(a) A grant is not normally repayable; a loan carries agreed repayment obligations. (b) An unused machine produces no expected return but adds debt service. Targeted assistance can make existing capacity usable, so amount alone does not establish a better productive effect.',['Point: repayment distinction [2].','Development: use unused capacity, obligations and the matched smaller input to compare the mechanism [4].']),
'P02':(['02-00','03-00'],'Describe (a) this fictional project comparison: A receives N$200,000 and reaches 30% of intended users; B receives N$150,000 with maintained local delivery and reaches 70% [3]. Suggest (b) the first issue to investigate in A if its aim is usable access and unfinished distribution is reported [3].','(a) B achieves coverage 40 points higher with less funding, so finance volume and reach do not rank alike. (b) Investigate distribution and maintenance responsibilities, not merely seek more funds; the reported delivery gap directly affects reach. The pair alone does not prove the reason for the difference.',['Evidence: compare amount and reach [3].','Meaning: justify a delivery investigation against the supplied fault and aim [3].'])}
